//! WHAT THE MACHINE INTERRUPTS FOR (design 178 D2 + D6; spec §7, §9): the
//! scheduler tick, the Timers that come due, a device line, and the idle poll.
//!
//! ONE COMPARATOR, TWO CUSTOMERS, and this is where they are composed: a fire
//! says only that something MAY be due, so every customer is asked out of ONE
//! reading of the clock and the tick is asked first.
//!
//! Everything here SERVICES state that lives below it — the timer slabs
//! (`time`), the interrupt slab (`waitables`), the ready queue (`threads`) —
//! and tells somebody through `wake`. The scheduler above it is what acts on
//! the flag a tick raises.

import hal
import kcore.limits.{MAX_INTERRUPTS, MAX_TIMERS, NO_INTERRUPT, NO_THREAD}
import kcore.diag.{console}
import kcore.threads.{
    CURRENT_THREAD,
    NEED_RESCHED,
    READY_HEAD,
    SLICE_LEFT,
    SLICE_TICKS,
}
import kcore.time.{NEXT_TICK_NS, TICK_PERIOD_NS, TIMERS, reprogram_timer}
import kcore.waitables.{INTERRUPTS, WaitableKind, interrupt_by_line}
import kcore.wake.{notify_ready}

/// Fire every Timer whose deadline `now` has reached.
///
/// AUDIT (design 1): NO PREEMPTION POINT — INELIGIBLE, and by definition. This
/// walk IS the IRQ path, so `IN_DELIVERY` is set for the whole of it and a point
/// written here would be inert anyway. Same for every loop it reaches: the wake
/// walks and the attachment unlinks under `notify_ready`. The bound is
/// `MAX_TIMERS` slots of O(1) work.
func expire_timers(now: UInt64) unsafe {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < MAX_TIMERS {
        match TIMERS[i].state {
            case Live -> {
                if TIMERS[i].is_due(now) {
                    fire_timer(i, now)
                }
            },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
}

/// One Timer came due: advance its schedule, and tell its Waiter.
///
/// TWO LINES, AND THE SPLIT IS THE POINT. `TimerSlot.fire` is the whole of the
/// drift-free re-arm and the coalescing division — arithmetic on one slot's own
/// fields, and a SAFE function for exactly that reason. What is left here is the
/// half that reaches the rest of the kernel: the slab index, and the wake.
func fire_timer(slot: Int, now: UInt64) unsafe {
    TIMERS[slot].fire(now)
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.Timer, slot)
}

// =============================================================================
// Interrupts (design 178 D2 + D6, as amended by design 1; spec §9)
// =============================================================================
//
// THE HARDWARE NEVER DELIVERS AN INTERRUPT IN KERNEL MODE; THE KERNEL ASKS, AT
// NAMED POINTS. That is design 178's D2 as design 1 (M3 unit 1.5) sharpened it,
// and the two halves are worth reading apart.
//
// THE FIRST HALF IS UNCHANGED AND STILL ENFORCED BY THE MACHINES. An interrupt
// is TAKEN from user mode and from nowhere else: the kernel runs with them
// masked from boot, each machine masks them again on trap entry in hardware, and
// each machine's return to user mode is where they come back on. Nothing below
// has to arrange any of that — it is a property of the two HALs, and each states
// in its ABI.md that an interrupt arriving in kernel mode lands on its
// kernel-bug path rather than on this code. So there are still no critical
// sections here, no interrupt masking vocabulary, and no `IntrSpinLock`
// (spec §9b) until SMP needs one.
//
// THE SECOND HALF IS WHAT DESIGN 1 ADDED: a long kernel operation POLLS. At a
// `preempt_point()` — below, beside the idle poll it is modelled on — the kernel
// asks the controller what is pending and services it synchronously through the
// same `deliver_line` the trap path uses. Semantics from the interrupt's side
// are the trap's: serviced at the point, the operation resumes. What it is NOT
// is a brief unmask, which is why the hardware enforcement above survives intact
// and goes on being a live tripwire rather than becoming a delivery path.
//
// SO THE M3 TRIPWIRE IS SPENT RATHER THAN RECORDED. Design 178 wrote down the
// cost — syscall latency bounds interrupt latency — and said to re-examine it
// when an M3 syscall grew a loop; the loops arrived, and this is the answer.
// What replaces the tripwire is a PLACEMENT RULE, one reviewable sentence per
// site: a point is legal where no in-flight invariant spans state the IRQ path
// touches. `IN_DELIVERY` below is that rule's mechanical backstop for the one
// place the rule cannot be read off a single function — the code the kernel's
// two contexts SHARE.
//
// WHAT IS ARCH-FREE HERE: which hook a line belongs to, what the kernel does
// with it, and the ORDER of claim / service / complete. What is not: how a line
// is claimed, masked and completed, and how a timer is rearmed — all six are
// seam calls, because a controller is a machine's, not a kernel's (D6). A point
// adds no seam: it is `irq_poll` again, which the idle path already needs.

/// How many of the first ticks the kernel narrates on the console.
///
/// A kernel that logged every tick would BE its own workload — at a
/// millisecond period the log is the machine — so the hook says enough to prove
/// the path ran and then goes quiet. The count keeps rising either way.
static TICK_REPORT_LIMIT: UInt = 4

/// Ticks the periodic timer has delivered since boot.
///
/// Compound global state resting on a serialization argument the compiler
/// cannot see, which is design 149's `unsafe static var`: uniprocessor kernel,
/// interrupts taken from user mode only, so the tick path is the only writer
/// and nothing can interleave with it.
///
/// BOTH COUNTERS BELOW WRAP ON PURPOSE (`&+`, not `+`). Overflow checks are
/// always on, so a plain `+` would make a full counter PANIC — and a kernel
/// that stops after seven weeks of uptime, or after four billion spurious
/// interrupts, has turned a number nobody was reading into an outage. A
/// wrapped count is what every tick counter does; deltas across a wrap are
/// still right, which is what the scheduler will ask of this one.
unsafe static var TIMER_TICKS: UInt = 0

/// Interrupts the controller had nothing to give for. ONE is not an error — a
/// line can stop asserting between raising the interrupt and being asked about
/// it — but a stream of them is, so the first is reported and the rest are
/// counted.
unsafe static var SPURIOUS_IRQS: UInt = 0

/// IS A DELIVERY IN FLIGHT? — the reentrancy guard for the code the kernel's two
/// contexts SHARE (design 1 D-2).
///
/// THE BYTE MOVERS SERVE TWO MASTERS. The loader's segment placement is
/// preemptible boot context and copies up to 240 KiB; `copy_out` runs in IRQ
/// CONTEXT — `wake.deliver_attachment` writes a parked thread's wait record from
/// the WAKER's context, and for an expired Timer that context is under
/// `deliver_line`. A preemption point inside a mover must therefore be INERT
/// when the mover is itself running under delivery, or a timer coming due during
/// that copy would re-enter delivery mid-delivery.
///
/// So the flag is set around `deliver_line`'s whole body and checked FIRST in
/// `preempt_point`. It is the MECHANICAL form of "no point runs inside the IRQ
/// path": it converts a placement error in shared code from a corruption into a
/// no-op, at ONE auditable site rather than as a convention every call site has
/// to remember. Every way into delivery funnels through `deliver_line` — the
/// trap path's `service_irq`, the idle poll, and now the preemption point — so
/// one flag covers all three.
///
/// Today's IRQ-path copies are wait records, 24 bytes, far under one stride, so
/// this is belt-and-braces at the moment; §2.1's Pipe `receive` is the length
/// that makes it load-bearing.
///
/// Compound global state resting on a serialization argument the compiler cannot
/// see, which is design 149's `unsafe static var`. The argument: uniprocessor
/// kernel, hardware delivery from user mode only, and the polled entries are
/// this flag's own readers — so the single kernel context is the only writer,
/// and no interleaving between the set and the clear is expressible. SMP is
/// where that ends, and the flag becomes per-core state behind the IntrSpinLock
/// spec §9b reserves.
public(package) unsafe static var IN_DELIVERY: Bool = false

/// Ticks the periodic timer has delivered since boot. The scheduler's clock
/// when there is a scheduler; today the harness's evidence that a tick was
/// taken, and when.
public func timer_ticks() unsafe -> UInt {
    TIMER_TICKS
}

/// THE TIMER INTERRUPT — the arch-free landing point for the one comparator.
///
/// A fire says only that SOMETHING MAY BE DUE, so every customer is asked out
/// of ONE reading of the clock, and the TICK IS ASKED FIRST — a timeslice charge
/// that a Timer's servicing delayed would be a scheduler bug wearing a Timer's
/// clothes. Programming the next deadline is last, and it is also what lowers
/// the line (both machines' timer condition is a level).
///
/// ONE `now` FOR BOTH is what makes the two customers unorderable in any way
/// that matters: the tick and every Timer are judged against the same instant,
/// so a Timer cannot be late because the tick ran first, and the tick cannot
/// slip because several Timers came due together.
///
/// `pc` is where the interrupted context resumes — or one of the two POLL
/// sentinels when nothing trapped: `IDLE_PC` when the idle path found the fire,
/// `PREEMPT_PC` when a preemption point did.
func on_timer_interrupt(pc: UInt) unsafe {
    let now = hal.timer_now_ns()

    if TICK_PERIOD_NS != 0 && now >= NEXT_TICK_NS {
        // Measured from NOW rather than from the deadline missed, which is M2's
        // rule kept verbatim: a tick the kernel was late for is ONE tick, never
        // a burst of catch-up ticks whose only effect would be to keep the
        // machine in the trap handler. A Timer's rule is the opposite (see
        // `fire_timer`) and deliberately so — a timeslice has no history to be
        // faithful to, and an interval does.
        NEXT_TICK_NS = now + TICK_PERIOD_NS
        on_timer_tick(pc)
    }

    expire_timers(now)
    reprogram_timer()
}

/// THE TIMER TICK — the arch-free landing point for the periodic timer.
///
/// THE TIMESLICE IS CHARGED HERE and nothing else about scheduling is: the tick
/// decrements the running thread's slice and, when it runs out, RAISES A FLAG.
/// It does not switch, does not walk the ready queue and does not touch the
/// frame. That division is design 178 D2 in one function — the tick runs with
/// interrupts masked, so its length is interrupt latency, while the switch it
/// motivates happens later at the user-return boundary where it costs nothing.
///
/// `pc` is where the interrupted context will resume, read out of the frame by
/// the caller — or a POLL SENTINEL when the tick was found rather than taken,
/// where there is no interrupted context to name: `IDLE_PC` from the idle path,
/// `PREEMPT_PC` from a preemption point. The report prints whichever, which is
/// what lets a transcript say which of the three deliveries ran.
func on_timer_tick(pc: UInt) unsafe {
    TIMER_TICKS = TIMER_TICKS &+ 1

    if CURRENT_THREAD != NO_THREAD {
        if SLICE_LEFT > 0 {
            SLICE_LEFT = SLICE_LEFT - 1
        }
        if SLICE_LEFT == 0 {
            NEED_RESCHED = true
            SLICE_LEFT = SLICE_TICKS
        }
    }

    if TIMER_TICKS > TICK_REPORT_LIMIT {
        return
    }
    let uart = console()
    uart.write_str("SOS: timer tick ")
    uart.write_hex(TIMER_TICKS)
    uart.write_str(" at ")
    uart.write_hex(pc)
    uart.write_str("\n")
}

/// What the tick report prints for a tick taken with nothing running. The
/// kernel was idle, so there is no interrupted address, and zero is the honest
/// answer rather than a stale one.
static IDLE_PC: UInt = 0

/// A DEVICE INTERRUPT — the arch-free landing point for an external line, and
/// spec §9's delivery in five lines.
///
/// THE LINE IS ALREADY MASKED when this runs (`deliver_line` below), so what is
/// left is to mark the object ready and tell its Waiter. Marking BEFORE waking
/// is what makes the level-triggered path work in both orders: a driver already
/// parked is woken with the record, and one that has not waited yet finds
/// `pending` set by its own next `Wait`.
///
/// A LINE NOTHING IS BOUND TO is reported and left MASKED, which is the only
/// safe answer: the kernel cannot service a device it has no driver for, and an
/// unmasked line with nobody to ack it would re-fire forever. That is also the
/// path the interrupt-controller bring-up case still exercises, which is what
/// keeps `irq_raise_selftest_line` meaningful with no Interrupt object involved.
func on_external_irq(line: UInt) unsafe {
    let irq = interrupt_by_line(line)
    if irq == NO_INTERRUPT {
        let uart = console()
        uart.write_str("SOS: external irq ")
        uart.write_hex(line)
        uart.write_str("\n")
        return
    }
    INTERRUPTS[irq].pending = true
    notify_ready(WaitableKind.Interrupt, irq)
}

/// SERVICE ONE CLAIMED LINE — the arch-free half of an interrupt, and the one
/// place a line number turns into work.
///
/// Its three entry points, all of which have already CLAIMED the line from the
/// controller:
///
///   - `service_irq`, from the trap path, with the interrupted PC;
///   - `idle_poll`, from `pick_next`'s idle loop, with `IDLE_PC`;
///   - `preempt_point`, from inside a long operation, with `PREEMPT_PC`.
///
/// THE GUARD IS HERE, AND IT WRAPS THE WHOLE BODY, because what it asserts is "a
/// delivery is in flight" and that is true from the claim to the completion. The
/// body is a function of its own for one reason: Saw has no `defer`, so a body
/// with an early return would need the clear written twice, and the second one
/// is the one a later edit forgets. One entry, one exit, one pair.
func deliver_line(line: UInt, pc: UInt) unsafe {
    IN_DELIVERY = true
    service_claimed_line(line, pc)
    IN_DELIVERY = false
}

/// The delivery itself, under the guard above and reached through nothing else.
///
/// Its two branches differ in exactly one step, and it is spec §9's: the timer
/// programs its NEXT DEADLINE, which is what lowers its line, while a device
/// line is MASKED, because a device goes on asserting until its driver has been
/// told.
func service_claimed_line(line: UInt, pc: UInt) unsafe {
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        SPURIOUS_IRQS = SPURIOUS_IRQS &+ 1
        if SPURIOUS_IRQS == 1 {
            console().write_str("SOS: spurious interrupt\n")
        }
        return
    }

    if line == hal.IRQ_TIMER {
        on_timer_interrupt(pc)
    } else {
        hal.irq_mask(line)
        on_external_irq(line)
    }
    hal.irq_complete(line)
}

/// EVERY interrupt the kernel TAKES arrives here, through `ktrap` and through
/// nothing else. (Interrupts the kernel POLLS FOR arrive through `idle_poll`
/// while it has nothing to run and through `preempt_point` while it is in the
/// middle of a long operation — the same service over two other ways of
/// noticing.)
public(package) func service_irq(frame_addr: UInt, cause: UInt) unsafe {
    deliver_line(hal.irq_claim(cause), hal.trap_pc(frame_addr))
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE IDLE PATH (spec §7; design 178 D6)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// "NOTHING RUNNABLE" USED TO BE A DEADLOCK, AND THE INTERRUPT OBJECT IS WHAT
// CHANGED THAT. Before it, every wake in the system came FROM a thread — an
// Event is signalled by somebody — so a system with no runnable thread had
// nobody left to signal one and the report was exact. A bound IRQ line is the
// first readiness that arrives from OUTSIDE the set of runnable threads, so the
// same state now has two readings and the kernel has to tell them apart.
//
// THE RULE: it is idle if any Interrupt is BOUND, and a deadlock otherwise. It
// is deliberately conservative — the kernel does not try to prove that a
// specific blocked thread is reachable by a specific line, which would mean
// walking attachment sets to answer a question that a wrong answer only makes
// SLOWER to diagnose (a hang at the harness timeout instead of a report). What
// it never does is call a real wait a deadlock.
//
// WHY THE IDLE LOOP POLLS INSTEAD OF TAKING THE INTERRUPT. Design 178's D2 says
// interrupts are taken from USER mode only, and that is enforced by the
// machines rather than intended by the kernel — so an interrupt that arrives
// while the kernel idles is not delivered, it is PENDING. `wait_for_irq` parks
// the core until one is pending (both machines wake from it on a pending
// interrupt whether or not it is deliverable), and `irq_poll` then asks the
// controller for it directly. So D2 is untouched: the kernel still never takes
// a trap in kernel mode, and the same `deliver_line` services the line either
// way.

/// Ask the controller for a line, and service it if there is one.
func idle_poll() unsafe {
    let line = hal.irq_poll()
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        return
    }
    deliver_line(line, IDLE_PC)
}

/// Is there a source of readiness OUTSIDE the set of runnable threads?
///
/// TWO KINDS ANSWER YES, and the second generalizes the rule rather than
/// widening it (design 232 unit 1). A BOUND IRQ LINE is readiness a device will
/// deliver; an ARMED TIMER is readiness the CLOCK will deliver. Neither needs a
/// thread to run for it to arrive, which is the whole of what this question
/// asks — so "idle iff a line is bound" was always the special case of "idle iff
/// something outside the thread set can still wake somebody".
///
/// Getting this wrong in the Timer's direction would be the worst kind of bug:
/// a process that armed a timer and parked is SLEEPING, which is the headline
/// thing this unit exists to make possible, and reporting it as a deadlock would
/// terminate exactly the program that finally works. Getting it wrong the other
/// way turns a real deadlock into a harness timeout, which is why an armed timer
/// counts and a merely EXISTING one does not: a disarmed Timer will never fire,
/// so it is not a wake source, and a system holding only those is as deadlocked
/// as one holding none.
public(package) func has_external_wake_source() unsafe -> Bool {
    var i: Int = 0
    while i < MAX_INTERRUPTS {
        match INTERRUPTS[i].state {
            case Live -> { return true },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    var t: Int = 0
    while t < MAX_TIMERS {
        match TIMERS[t].state {
            case Live -> {
                if TIMERS[t].armed {
                    return true
                }
            },
            case Free -> {},
        }
        t = t + 1
    }
    false
}

/// Park the core until something is runnable again.
///
/// The exit condition is the READY QUEUE, not the poll: a serviced line wakes a
/// waiter through `deliver_line` -> `on_external_irq` -> `notify_ready`, which
/// pushes that thread onto the queue. A line that wakes nobody — one bound to
/// an Interrupt no thread is waiting on — leaves the queue empty and the loop
/// goes back to sleep, which is correct and is why the test is on the queue
/// rather than on "an interrupt happened".
///
/// AUDIT (design 1): NO POINT NEEDED — this loop IS delivery, and the two
/// bounded scans it sits beside (`has_external_wake_source` over the interrupt
/// and timer slabs) are the same shape as every other bounded-tiny slab walk in
/// the kernel: `MAX_INTERRUPTS` + `MAX_TIMERS` iterations of O(1) work.
public(package) func idle_until_runnable() unsafe {
    while READY_HEAD == 0 {
        hal.wait_for_irq()
        idle_poll()
    }
}

// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE PREEMPTION POINT (design 1, M3 unit 1.5)
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// IT IS THE IDLE POLL WITH A DIFFERENT REASON FOR ASKING, and that is the whole
// of design 1's D-1. The idle path polls because the kernel has nothing to run;
// a long operation polls because the kernel has been running too long. Both ask
// the same seam, service through the same funnel, and leave the hardware rule
// (interrupts are TAKEN from user mode only) exactly where it was — which is
// what design 1 chose over briefly unmasking, since an unmask needs a kernel-mode
// trap path on both machines and spends the tripwire that today catches a kernel
// bug on both.
//
// WHAT A POINT INTERRUPTS IS LATENCY, NOT ATOMICITY. A reschedule the delivery
// signals — NEED_RESCHED from a tick, a wake from an expired Timer — is honored
// where it always was, at the user-return boundary through `pick_next`. The
// operation still runs to completion before the switch it may have motivated.
//
// WHERE THE POINTS THEMSELVES ARE: `kcore.preempt`, which is one module above
// this one and holds the strided long-op movers that call this. They are not
// here, and they are not in `mem` beside the byte loops they drive, for a reason
// worth stating once — see that module's header.

/// The sentinel a tick report prints for a tick taken at a preemption point.
///
/// A DISTINCT VALUE RATHER THAN A SHARED `IDLE_PC`, decided here because design 1
/// left it to the implementation and diagnosability decides it: the two polled
/// deliveries are the two ways the kernel notices an interrupt nothing trapped it
/// into, and a transcript that cannot tell "the kernel was idle" from "the kernel
/// was halfway through a 240 KiB copy" has lost the very fact this unit exists to
/// make visible. One costs nothing and is safe for the same reason zero is: an
/// ODD address is not an instruction address on either profile — both machines
/// fetch on at least a two-byte boundary — so it can never collide with a real
/// interrupted PC.
static PREEMPT_PC: UInt = 1

/// A PREEMPTION POINT — the kernel asking, mid-operation, whether the machine
/// wants anything (design 1 D-1).
///
/// THE GUARD IS FIRST and it is not an optimization: `IN_DELIVERY` set means this
/// call is already inside the IRQ path, where a second delivery would re-enter
/// the tables the first one is halfway through. Returning is the whole handling —
/// the interrupt stays pending and is taken at the next legal moment, which is
/// what "the point is inert here" means.
///
/// THE CALLER OWES THE PLACEMENT SENTENCE. This function cannot check that no
/// in-flight invariant spans state the IRQ path touches; it is a property of
/// where the call is written, so every call site carries the one-line verdict
/// design 1's audit table records.
public(package) func preempt_point() unsafe {
    if IN_DELIVERY {
        return
    }
    let line = hal.irq_poll()
    if line == hal.IRQ_NONE {
        return
    }
    deliver_line(line, PREEMPT_PC)
}
